Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: draw guest frames on flush; opt-in IOSurface presenter

The GL display drew a guest frame only after the next GUI refresh tick and
Core Animation's display callback: 6-8 ms from the guest's flush to the draw
at 120 Hz (p95 11-13 ms), and 70-80% of 120 fps guest output was drawn.

* Request the layer draw as soon as the guest flushes (Core Animation still
  draws at most once per display refresh; a hidden window never draws).
  Measured: flush-to-draw p50 0.14 ms, p95 ~2 ms, every frame drawn at 60
  and 120 fps, 4K60 playback power within the earlier spread.
* COCOA_GL_PRESENT=iosurface: copy each frame into one of three IOSurfaces
  on the QEMU thread and only swap the layer contents on the main thread
  (no BQL there).  p50 0.12 ms, p95 0.25 ms, no torn frames in 1,200
  alternating-colour frames, but 4K60 playback cost 0.8 W more in an ABBA
  comparison, so it is opt-in.  Apple's GL accepts but crashes on
  lossless-compressed (&BGA) render targets, so compression is not an option.
* Measurement aids: COCOA_FLOAT=1 keeps the window on screen on every Space
  (accessory app, floating level) for display benchmarks;
  COCOA_GL_PRESENT_CHECK=1 counts non-uniform frames (IOSurface path);
  COCOA_GL_PRESENT_DUMP=<file.ppm> writes one presented frame.
* Link IOSurface.framework for the Cocoa UI.

